# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# MUST STAY LAST. Git applies the LAST matching pattern, so these negations only
# hold if nothing below re-excludes the path. Placed mid-file they silently lost to
# `settings.json`, `/build*` and `.vscode` further down -- *.d recovered, the rest
# did not, which is worse than not trying because it looks fixed.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ── Vendored upstream fixtures must not be eaten by OUR build-artifact patterns ──
# corpus/programbench/per_tool_overrides/ is a deliberately tracked tree: 124,376 files
# are already committed. But 1,094 files inside it were being ignored (measured
# 2026-07-29 by scripts/dev/gitignore_audit.py), because patterns written for this
# project's own output happen to match UPSTREAM names:
#
#   *.d            1083 files -- meant for C dependency files, but ctags' test suite uses
#                  DIRECTORIES named *.d (Tmain/list-roles.d/), so every fixture inside
#                  one vanished
#   fasttext          7 -- a bare name intended as a local artifact dir, matching
#                  fasttext's own python module tree
#   settings.json     1 -- meant as VS Code's, matched duckdb's src/common/settings.json
#   verify_*.json     1 -- matched duckdb's test config
#   .vscode           1 -- matched an upstream extension's own .vscode
#   /build*, /*build* 2 -- matched upstream build.sh scripts
#
# Total 733 KB, i.e. a rounding error against the tree it belongs to -- while a fresh
# clone got that tree with 1,094 holes in it, which is exactly the kind of silent
# incompleteness that makes a campaign non-reproducible.
!corpus/programbench/per_tool_overrides/**

# SwiftPM build locks. These hold nothing but a PID, and three of them were tracked by accident --
# so `swift build` inside a fixture (which the test suite does) rewrote them and left `git status`
# dirty after a plain `pytest`. A contributor on a clean checkout then cannot tell whether they
# broke something.
#
# Deliberately placed AFTER the !per_tool_overrides negation above: later patterns win, and one of
# the three sat under that force-included tree. That negation exists so a fresh clone gets the
# complete source tree; a PID lock file is a build artifact, not source, so excluding it serves the
# same intent rather than reopening a hole.
#
# `**/` is load-bearing: a pattern with a mid-path slash is anchored to this file's directory, so a
# bare `.build/.lock` matches only a repo-root one and left all three nested files un-ignored.
# Confirmed with `git check-ignore -v`, which still reported the negation as the winning rule.
**/.build/.lock
